# Tauri / Rust build output
/target/
/gen/

# Cargo.lock is TRACKED, deliberately. This is a binary that ships to users, and the root
# Cargo.toml's own rule — "Dependency versions are pinned here for the whole workspace … Bumps
# are single dedicated commits, never mixed into a feature task" — cannot hold for `app` while
# its lockfile is ignored: the graph re-resolves on every build and nobody reviews the change.
#
# It also broke CI caching in a way that hid itself. The rust-cache key is hashed from
# Cargo.TOML's caret ranges, so when an upstream crate cut a patch release cargo re-resolved,
# the restored artifacts stopped fingerprint-matching and the 538-crate tree rebuilt — but the
# key was unchanged, so the action logged "full match: true" and skipped saving. The six-minute
# rebuild then repeated on every subsequent run until somebody happened to edit Cargo.toml.

# Frontend build output + deps
/ui/node_modules/
/ui/dist/
/ui/package-lock.json
/ui/bun.lock

# App icons. `cargo tauri icon` generates a whole set here (icon.icns, Square*Logo.png, …) and
# those are build output — but the handful below are COMMITTED, because they are the source the
# bundlers read and there is no high-resolution original in the tree to regenerate them from.
#
# They used to be tracked *despite* this rule, which is a state git allows and nobody can see: the
# files were force-added once, so they kept working, and `icon.ico` — never added — looked like it
# was simply absent. It was, and Windows release builds failed on it (`icons/icon.ico` not found;
# required for generating a Windows Resource file during tauri-build) while every other platform
# built fine. Listing the committed set explicitly is what makes a missing one visible.
/icons/*
!/icons/32x32.png
!/icons/128x128.png
!/icons/icon.png
!/icons/icon.ico
